{"repositoryId":754825051,"availability":"available","full":"rameel/ramstack.htmxtoolkit","name":"ramstack.htmxtoolkit","description":"HTMX for ASP.NET Core: typed HX-* headers, fluent responses, Tag Helpers, and antiforgery. Supports HTMX 1.9, 2, and 4.","category":"Developer tools","language":"C#","stars":14,"forks":0,"created":"2024-02-08T20:44:49Z","updated":"2026-10-12T22:19:08Z","license":"MIT","branch":"main","homepage":"https://rameel.github.io/ramstack.htmxtoolkit/","topics":["aspnetcore","htmx","minimal-api","razor","razor-pages","tag-helpers"],"demo":"https://rameel.github.io/ramstack.htmxtoolkit/","color":"#323057","ink":"#d6caff","lastCheckedAt":"2026-10-13T07:38:05.568Z","lastAttemptAt":"2026-10-13T07:38:05.568Z","lastAvailableAt":"2026-10-13T07:38:05.568Z","metrics":[{"at":"2026-10-13T07:38:05.568Z","stars":14,"forks":0}],"technologies":[],"overview":{"kind":"readme","version":1,"paragraphs":["HtmxToolkit helps you build HTMX applications with ASP.NET Core.","HTMX and the server talk through HX- headers. Requests carry details such as the target element and the current URL, and your response can tell HTMX where to put the HTML, whether to change the URL, or which events to raise. HtmxToolkit lets you do all of this from C# and Razor, without header strings or hand-written JSON:","HtmxToolkit runs on .NET 6 or later and supports HTMX 1.9.x, 2.x, and 4.x. HTMX 2.x is the default."],"features":["Find out whether HTMX expects a fragment or a full page, and read its headers as typed properties.","Tell HTMX what to do next with a fluent API: retarget, reswap, redirect, push a URL, or trigger an event.","Send HTMX requests to dedicated MVC actions with [HtmxRequest].","Build hx-get and hx-post URLs from controllers, actions, and Razor Pages, just like the asp- Tag Helpers do.","Configure HTMX once in Program.cs and send antiforgery tokens with HTMX requests automatically."],"sourceUrl":"https://github.com/rameel/ramstack.htmxtoolkit/blob/main/README.md","fin